WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér. Dr. Papers.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:13.020
Today you are going to see an insane paper. One of&nbsp;
the most fun papers I’ve read in a while. Earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:19.680
scientists at DeepMind were looking to teach&nbsp;
a bunch of AI agents to play football/soccer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:26.520
Unfortunately, they started out like this.&nbsp;
Not very impressive. But then, they trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:34.440
them for 5 years. What? 5 years? Well, their&nbsp;
training took what felt like 5-years for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:41.820
but only 3 days for us, because these are not&nbsp;
real robots, and thus, on a fast computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:47.760
they can be simulated faster than real&nbsp;
time. So what happened in that time?

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.660
This happened! Whoa! Now they can&nbsp;
not only play amazingly well, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.880
they can even think a few steps ahead,&nbsp;
and they are even anticipating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.080
behavior of their teammates and&nbsp;
position themselves accordingly.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.540
The saves they are capable of are&nbsp;
also outstanding. And as we don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.880
really have a referee around, they also&nbsp;
beat up each other. And when I say that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:22.620
I really mean it. It’s bad. Real bad. Also,&nbsp;
the only thing they like more than throwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:28.500
each other is throwing tantrums. And&nbsp;
it even gets worse. This lovely chap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:34.500
almost seems like someone who is faking an&nbsp;
injury, but then, look! A miracle happened!

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:40.320
And today, scientists at DeepMind&nbsp;
brought this work to the next level. How?

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.740
In a different paper, scientists at OpenAI&nbsp;
simulated a robot hand in a video game world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:53.580
Then, the things it learned in this simulation&nbsp;
could be brought into life by deploying it into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.580 --> 00:02:01.800
the real world. So train in a simulation, and then&nbsp;
play in the real world. Yes, that is fantastic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:09.240
but, why am I saying this? Oh wait, is is&nbsp;
possible that the same happened here too? Real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:16.500
robots playing football? Yes! That is exactly what&nbsp;
happened. So with that, let the insanity begin.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:24.300
The new environment contains 2 real robots with&nbsp;
20 actuated joints each. So what did they learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:32.220
first? Well, first, they learned to get up, and&nbsp;
also, score goals. All this in a simulated robot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:39.780
body. That is very impressive already, but wait,&nbsp;
because that’s just the start. Now it is time for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:45.900
random perturbations. What does that mean? It&nbsp;
means that forces are randomly exerted on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:54.120
AI to make sure it can recover from an impact.&nbsp;
And, are you thinking what I am thinking? Oh yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:01.680
now let’s go to the real robots, and let’s test&nbsp;
that impact. Aww! That is not very nice. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:09.780
then…wow! Look at that! What it had learned&nbsp;
in a simulation now really applies to a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:16.980
life robot. It can get hit, and it is able to&nbsp;
get up over and over again. My goodness. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:24.540
there is a problem. What is the problem? Well,&nbsp;
their knees get injured all the time. So what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.760
did scientists at DeepMind do with that?&nbsp;
Well, hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:37.080
because they taught the robot to get up and move&nbsp;
in a way that is not too hard on the knees. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:42.840
that’s right! And thus, they didn’t suffer that&nbsp;
many knee injuries. What a time to be alive!

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:51.000
So with that, finally, they can now run, and&nbsp;
score goals. But wait, playing a match and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.780
winning takes more than just running and kicking.&nbsp;
They need to have a variety of other skills and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:05.580
combine them in order to win. So, did they? Oh&nbsp;
yes they did! They can now control the ball,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:11.220
turn with it, block the ball with their&nbsp;
bodies, shoot accurately, even in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:17.400
case of a moving ball, which is even harder.&nbsp;
And that is the miracle of research papers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.440
such incredible progress can happen&nbsp;
just one more paper down the line.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:27.420
And, yes, I know, I know. You are&nbsp;
asking the question, okay, but Károly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:34.260
did they beat up each other here too like in the&nbsp;
previous paper? And the answer is no. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:39.240
they are penalized for getting too close to&nbsp;
each other, so luckily that did not happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:46.200
But this kind of improvement in just one paper, I&nbsp;
am wondering what these little AIs will be capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.960
of two more papers down the line? Well, what do&nbsp;
you think? Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:50.340
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

